{
 "name": "card-vault",
 "owner": "card-vault team",
 "updated": "2026-10-02",
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Overview",
   "text": "The card vault stores tokenized card data. payments-api calls it on every checkout to exchange a card token for a network token. It runs in all three regions, behind an internal load balancer, and enforces mTLS. Typical latency is 15 ms at p50 and 60 ms at p99."
  },
  {
   "title": "Symptoms",
   "text": "Checkout 502s, CardVaultClient timeouts, card vault 429s, or a growing number of pending connections on the client side. Client-side pool exhaustion is usually a client configuration issue, not a vault outage: check the vault's own dashboards before paging."
  },
  {
   "title": "First steps",
   "text": "1. Check recent payments-api deployments. 2. If a release changed the card vault client, roll it back first; do not tune pool sizes during an incident. 3. Compare the vault's request rate with the client's. 4. If vault latency stays above 200 ms at p99 after a rollback, page the card vault team."
  },
  {
   "title": "Draining traffic",
   "text": "Traffic can be shifted away from one region with the regional weight in the global load balancer (payments-lb). Shift in steps of 25% and watch the error rate of the remaining regions. Never drain two regions at the same time."
  },
  {
   "title": "Escalation",
   "text": "Card vault team on-call this rotation: Aiko Tanaka, reachable in #cardvault-oncall or through the pager policy CARDVAULT-P1. Security incidents go to the security on-call instead."
  },
  {
   "title": "Known issues",
   "text": "The vault rejects more than 400 concurrent connections per client certificate. Clients must keep their pool well below that limit. Certificate rotation happens on the first Monday of each month at 06:00 UTC and can cause a few seconds of handshake errors."
  }
 ]
}

